1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,170
Listen to a talk on a podcast about music.

2
00:00:04,170 --> 00:00:08,862
Today, let's explore the fascinating world of synaesthesia,

3
00:00:08,862 --> 00:00:14,140
a condition where one sensory experience involuntarily triggers another.

4
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:18,077
This phenomenon is particularly intriguing in the realm of music

5
00:00:18,659 --> 00:00:21,230
where certain individuals can see sounds or taste music

6
00:00:21,230 --> 00:00:25,080
Synaesthetic experiences vary widely among individuals.

7
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:28,060
For example, some might perceive specific colors

8
00:00:28,060 --> 00:00:31,080
when hearing particular musical notes or chords.

9
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:36,210
A person might see red when they hear a C note or blue when they hear a G note.

10
00:00:36,210 --> 00:00:40,883
This blending of senses can enhance their emotional connection to the music

11
00:00:40,883 --> 00:00:43,220
and even influence their creative processes.

12
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:47,102
A number of famous musicians, such as Duke Ellington,

13
00:00:47,102 --> 00:00:53,140
have reported experiencing synesthesia which they claim helps them in composing and performing music.

14
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:59,580
Neurologists theorize that synaesthesia stems from heightened neural connections between sensory regions

15
00:01:00,180 --> 00:01:04,140
allowing stimulation of one sense to trigger involuntary experiences with another.

16
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:07,920
Though uncommon, it offers profound insight into perception

17
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:11,165
and the mechanisms underlying sensory integration,

18
00:01:11,165 --> 00:01:15,180
helping researchers explore cognition, memory and even creativity.

19
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:18,260
Moreover, synesthesia is not limited to music.

20
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:21,328
It can involve other combinations of senses,

21
00:01:21,328 --> 00:01:26,150
such as associating certain tastes with numbers or textures with emotions.

22
00:01:26,150 --> 00:01:30,860
Understanding synaesthesia can help us appreciate the varied ways people experience the world

23
00:01:30,860 --> 00:01:34,120
and highlight the incredible potential of the human brain.
